{
 "thesis": "Jazz pianist Phil Howe rejects teaching modes as rotations starting on different white keys (C Ionian, D Dorian, E Phrygian...) and instead teaches a mirror trick that keeps the root fixed on C and swaps in a different key signature for each mode, played live at an outdoor night market gig.",
 "summary": "Phil argues the standard academic method of walking modes up the white keys (C Ionian, D Dorian, E Phrygian, F Lydian...) is useless because the ear only ever hears the underlying C major scale in context, never the mode's own character. His fix, demonstrated on a keyboard at a string-lit street event: keep every mode rooted on C, and find its key signature by reflecting the scale degree that named the mode (D, E, F, G, A, B) across C, then borrowing that mirrored note's major key signature to play from C. He walks through it live: C Dorian using the Bb key signature (mirror of D), C Phrygian using the Ab key signature (mirror of E), C Lydian and C Mixolydian using signatures mirrored from F and G, C natural minor (Aeolian) using the Eb key signature (mirror of A), and finally Locrian from B's mirror. On-screen captions track each mode name (Ionian, Dorian, Phrygian, Mixolydian, minor, B/Locrian) as he plays it, ending with a \"tune in next time\" sign-off card for the \"Learn How with Phil Howe\" series.",
 "key_concepts": [
