{
 "thesis": "Any periodic (or, in the limit, non-periodic) signal can be decomposed into sine and cosine components whose amplitudes are found by multiplying the signal by a probe sine/cosine and integrating over one period, a technique Fourier invented specifically to solve the heat equation.",
 "summary": "The video reconstructs Fourier's reasoning as a Socratic dialogue: starting from the single fact that heat flows from hot to cold, it derives the 1D heat equation (rate of temperature change equals curvature, i.e. the second spatial derivative) and shows that sine profiles are the easiest case because a sine's curvature is proportional to its own height, so a sine simply decays exponentially in place, with higher frequencies decaying faster. Fourier's leap is that any shape can be built from sine waves, letting each frequency evolve independently under the heat equation and then be summed. To find the amplitude of each frequency component, the video walks through the sine/cosine multiply-and-integrate trick (orthogonality of sinusoids), and handles phase shift by computing both sine and cosine projections and combining them via the Pythagorean theorem. It extends this from periodic Fourier series to the continuous Fourier transform by taking the period to infinity, then covers real applications: Lord Kelvin's tide-predicting machines (used for both British sea trade and D-Day invasion planning), audio/image/video compression via discarding imperceptible frequencies, and the cochlea in the human ear acting as a physical Fourier analyzer via stiff-to-flexible oscillators.",
